 ## Overview
-View-dependent color with Spherical Harmonics (SH) is a technique used in 3D Gaussian Splatting to model the view-dependent appearance (e.g., reflections, highlights) of each Gaussian primitive by representing its color as a function of the viewing direction [S1, unofficial][S2]. Unlike static color models that store a single RGB value per Gaussian, this method stores a set of coefficients that, when evaluated against the camera-to-Gaussian direction, produce a directionally varying color [S2][S5, unofficial]. This approach solves the problem of capturing specular details and reflections in point-based rendering without requiring per-ray neural network queries, keeping the rendering path closed-form and computationally cheap [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Ray-marching on 3D Gaussians](1036-ray-marching-on-3d-gaussians.md) by providing the appearance model for the primitives being marched.
